{"internal_reasoning": "Now the offer is symmetrical: I cover your cost at (1,1), and you cover my cost at (1,0). This means: Normally, I would spend R when stepping on (1,0) anyway, so if you cover it, I save 1 R. In exchange, I pay 1 R for you at (1,1), which I otherwise would not spend. My net R change: +1 saved from your coverage, -1 spent to cover you. Net 0 R difference. I gain no chip surplus compared to walking solo. 

Their earlier message says they're willing to cover ANY one R tile along my path. If they cover (3,2) or (3,1) instead, it does not make a difference to me than (1,0), since I must spend R on all moves. There is still no net gain unless I can negotiate for coverage on more than one R tile (ideally, save more R than I pay), but that is not currently available.

I lose nothing by agreeing to symmetrical exchange, but I also do not benefit. Since my stated strategy is to ONLY agree if I strictly benefit, I must decline, unless a new offer is possible. Let me politely but clearly decline.", "external_message": "Thanks for the revised offer! However, exchanging coverage of one R tile each does not give me any chip surplus compared to walking alone: I'd save 1R from your coverage and spend 1R covering you, so my final count is identical to a solo walk. Since I'd only cooperate if I strictly benefit, I must decline this proposal. If you're open to covering more than one R tile for me or otherwise improving my chip surplus, let me know."}
